# Environment variables for Claude Code cloud sessions.
#
# Paste the two uncommented lines at the bottom into claude.ai/code → environment settings
# → Environment variables, with your project's real values. The format is .env: one
# KEY=value per line, no quotes needed, and a `#` in an unquoted value starts a comment.
#
# Each session copies these once at startup. Editing them affects sessions you start
# afterwards, not ones already running.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What must never go here
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A cloud environment has no secrets store. Every value is readable in plain text by
# anyone who can use the environment, and it is copied into ordinary environment variables
# that every command in the session can read. So these three, from .env.example, stay out:
#
#   SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY     bypasses every RLS policy - full read/write on every યુવક's
#                           data. It belongs in Netlify → Site settings → Environment
#                           variables, where only netlify/functions/* read it at runtime.
#   SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD    the Postgres password, used by scripts/db.mjs to apply
#                           migrations. Pass it from your own shell for a single command.
#   SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY / SUPABASE_URL
#                           the unprefixed server-side pair read by
#                           netlify/functions/login-mobile.js. Nothing in a cloud session
#                           needs them, so there is no reason to expose them.
#
# .github/workflows/ci.yml draws the same line for the same reason: nothing CI runs should
# be able to read production data. A cloud session is a machine you do not have a shell
# on, running an agent - hold it to at least that standard.
#
# The practical cost is small. Everything that needs a secret is out of reach in a cloud
# session anyway:
#
#   npm test                8 suites over shared/domain/* - pure functions, no env, no
#                           network. Works with nothing configured at all.
#   npm run build           needs the two public VITE_ values below.
#   npm run verify:*        needs the same two, plus the Chrome that
#                           scripts/cloud-setup.sh installs.
#   npm run test:rls        needs Docker only - it builds its own throwaway postgres:16.
#   npm run seed:admin      needs SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY. Run it locally, not in the cloud.
#   node scripts/db.mjs     needs SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD. Run it locally, not in the cloud.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What goes here
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both of these are PUBLIC by design. The publishable key ships to every visitor's browser
# and identifies the project; it grants nothing on its own. Row Level Security is what
# protects the data, which is why every table has policies rather than relying on key
# secrecy - and why scripts/test-rls.mjs exists to prove those policies still hold.
#
# They are required rather than optional: src/lib/supabase.js builds its client at module
# scope and shared/supabase/client.js throws when either is missing, so a bundle built
# without them crashes on load. scripts/install_pkgs.sh copies them into .env.local at
# session start.
#
# Copy from Supabase → Project Settings → API keys, the same pair as .env.local.

VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...
